@{
    ViewData["Title"] = T("Security Whitepaper") + " — DashLane";
    Layout = "_AuthLayout";
    ViewData["MainClass"] = "legal-main";
}

<div class="legal-page">
    <article class="legal-card">
        <p class="legal-kicker">DashLane Mobility</p>
        <h1 class="legal-title">@T("Security Whitepaper")</h1>
        <p class="legal-updated">@T("Last updated: November 10, 2026")</p>
        @if (IsFrench)
        {
            <p class="notice legal-lang-note">@T("This document is currently available in English only.")</p>
        }
        <p class="legal-lead">
            This page describes the security controls that exist in the current DashLane build.
            It does not claim certifications or security products that are not implemented.
        </p>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>1. Scope</h2>
            <p>
                DashLane today covers account creation, cookie sign-in, and a five-step onboarding flow.
                Ride marketplace, payments, and messaging are out of scope until they are built.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>2. Authentication</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Email and password sign-in uses ASP.NET Core cookie authentication.</li>
                <li>Passwords are hashed with ASP.NET Core <code>PasswordHasher</code> before storage.</li>
                <li>Failed sign-in returns a generic message so the response does not reveal whether the email exists.</li>
                <li>The auth cookie is HTTP-only and uses sliding expiration. “Remember this device” can keep the session for 14 days.</li>
                <li>Google OAuth uses official Google authentication when ClientId and ClientSecret are configured. Without those values, the Google buttons report that sign-in is unavailable.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>3. Data storage</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Member records live in SQLite at <code>App_Data/dashlane.db</code>.</li>
                <li>Email addresses are stored normalized and unique.</li>
                <li>Uploaded photos and documents are saved under <code>App_Data/members/{id}/</code>, outside <code>wwwroot</code>.</li>
                <li>Profile photos are returned only to the signed-in owner through a controller action.</li>
                <li>Document files are not publicly linked from profile pages.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>4. Phone verification</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Phone numbers are stored after digit normalization.</li>
                <li>One-time codes are generated, stored, expired, and rate-limited by Twilio Verify; DashLane only stores whether the number is verified.</li>
                <li>Twilio credentials live in <code>.env</code> (<code>TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID</code>, <code>TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN</code>, <code>TWILIO_VERIFY_SERVICE_SID</code>). If they are missing, sending fails with an error; the code is never displayed in the page.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>5. Automatic document checks</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>When <code>IdCheck__IdAnalyzerApiKey</code> is set, uploaded JPG, PNG, and WebP documents are sent over HTTPS to ID Analyzer's <code>/scan</code> API with the member's legal name and date of birth.</li>
                <li>Only the decision (accept, review, or reject), a short summary, and the transaction reference are stored. ID Analyzer's own retention settings apply to data sent to it.</li>
                <li>PDF uploads and uploads made while the key is missing are not checked automatically and are left for manual review.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>6. Uploads</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Photos accept JPEG, PNG, and WebP.</li>
                <li>Documents also accept PDF.</li>
                <li>Uploads are limited to 10 MB.</li>
                <li>Saved paths are checked so files cannot leave the member’s own folder.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>7. Application protections</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Form posts that change account data use anti-forgery tokens.</li>
                <li>Onboarding pages after signup require an authenticated cookie.</li>
                <li>Passwords are never written to browser <code>localStorage</code> or <code>sessionStorage</code> by DashLane scripts.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>8. What this build does not provide</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>No ISO/IEC 27001 certification claim</li>
                <li>No face match or liveness check (the automatic ID check reads the document only)</li>
                <li>No member-to-member SMS relay or virtual telephony bridge</li>
                <li>No production hosting hardening beyond the local development server</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Those items can be added later with real providers and an updated whitepaper.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>9. Responsible use of the local database</h2>
            <p>
                Anyone with filesystem access to the server can read <code>App_Data</code>.
                Use this build on trusted machines, keep the database out of public repositories, and rotate credentials if the database file is copied.
            </p>
        </section>

        <nav class="legal-nav" aria-label="Related policies">
            <a class="legal-nav-link" href="@Url.Action("Terms", "Home")">@T("Terms of Service")</a>
            <a class="legal-nav-link" href="@Url.Action("Privacy", "Home")">@T("Privacy Policy")</a>
            <a class="legal-nav-link" href="@Url.Action("Index", "Home")">@T("Back to welcome")</a>
        </nav>
    </article>
</div>
